[stderr] Create the matching machine-readable
[stderr] `planning/game-production-inventory.json`. Each entry identifies its design
[stderr] unit, selected module, content/design-card type, finite item or catalogue
[stderr] batch, planned quantity, original brief, dependencies, asset/audio IDs,
[stderr] implementation outcome, owner, and verification. Every selected module and
[stderr] every `GAME-*` unit must have at least one inventory entry. A finite RPG roster
[stderr] therefore names and describes its actual first-release party, bosses, towns,
[stderr] quests, or items as appropriate; a football-trivia catalogue instead has
[stderr] explicit category/difficulty/type quotas and small editorial batches with
[stderr] source validation. Do not use vague entries such as “make game content.”
[stderr] 
[stderr] For a game, also create `projects/<project-slug>/game-design-runbooks/`, then
[stderr] write `GD0001.md`: a bounded game-design-programme dispatcher. Register this as
[stderr] the `game-design-runbooks` project collection. It inherits the project's
[stderr] existing `PROJECT_BRIEF.md` and `INITIAL.md`; never create or duplicate an
[stderr] `INITIAL.md` inside this collection.
[stderr] GD0001 reads the discovery, identity, initial design bible, creative direction,
[stderr] and production inventory; it writes `planning/game-design-manifest.json` and
[stderr] creates at most ten `GB` bounded design-runbook writers, one `GC` catalogue
[stderr] checkpoint, and one successor `GD` dispatcher when more work remains. Each
[stderr] `GB` writer creates at most **five** focused `G` design runbooks. Those G files
[stderr] create/review the detailed bibles required by the selected modules (not a fixed
[stderr] RPG list), such as story/world/party/boss design for an RPG or question
[stderr] bank/scoring/fairness design for trivia. `GC` validates G ownership, design-unit
[stderr] coverage, dependency closure, output quality, and manifest consistency before a
[stderr] later GD wave continues. GD dispatchers add further bounded waves until every
[stderr] required design module and production entry has completed design evidence, then
[stderr] create a final `GQ` design audit.
[stderr] The final audit must update the canonical bible and inventory, run the
[stderr] dedicated manifest completion check, and leave a completed manifest. The full
[stderr] implementation-coverage validator runs later, after F012 creates its catalogue.
[stderr] Do not generate B or R implementation runbooks until the G collection has
[stderr] passed.
[stderr] 
[stderr] The manifest is a completion gate, not a prose report. Its required fields are
[stderr] `status: accepted`, `final_audit` (`task_id: G...`, `status: accepted`), and a
[stderr] `modules` array. Each selected module records `module`, `status: accepted`,
[stderr] `design_output_paths`, `game_design_ids`, and `production_inventory_ids`.
[stderr] It also records empty `pending_modules` and `blocked_modules` arrays. The final
[stderr] audit may set these values only after it has checked all generated G evidence;
[stderr] the Supervisor and `scripts/runbookgen validate` reject any other form. The
[stderr] manifest module set must exactly match `02-game-design-bible.json`, and each
[stderr] module must cover all of its canonical `GAME-*` design-unit IDs before F006 can
[stderr] start.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Every generated `G0001`, `G0002`, … design task uses normal Supervisor task
[stderr] metadata and declares `design_authoring_batch: GB0001` (or its actual GB
[stderr] writer). G tasks are the actual game-design work; GB/GC/GD/GQ files are only
[stderr] the scalable authoring, checking, dispatch, and audit layer that lets hundreds
[stderr] of detailed G files be created without a single giant prompt.
